## Design report

Your research report is `report/paper.pdf`, and it is entirely yours. The document section above names it and how to compile it; nothing else writes any part of it. It is a sibling of the generated package, never a file inside it:

```text
search/           the generated Search, executable Python only
└── <your package source>
report/
├── paper.tex        the complete document, \documentclass to \end{document}
├── paper.pdf        what your compile produces; this is the design report
├── references.bib   only if you cite with BibTeX
└── figures/         PNG only, and only when a figure carries a real argument
```

The package carries no `README.md` and no document of its own: `search/` holds importable Python and nothing else. The submission gate refuses your Output until `report/paper.pdf` exists, and nothing will compile the report for you afterwards.

Write a decision-oriented paper, not a log of what you did. Keep the evidence, findings, interpretations, rejected alternatives, and assumptions that materially support the final Search, and leave out the command history. Use this structure:

1. Abstract
2. Introduction
3. Evidence and Prior Work -- always present.
4. Preliminary Investigation -- only when this invocation produced new dataset analysis, pilots, or other new evidence. Omit it otherwise; never write an empty ceremonial section.
5. Methodology, covering the Search strategy, the execution topology, the execution stages, the control algorithm, and the resource and budget plan.
6. Evaluation
7. Limitations and Adaptation
8. Conclusion

The Search strategy explains why this approach follows from the evidence and why the obvious alternatives were rejected. The resource and budget plan explains concurrency, capabilities, expected scale, the live remainder check, the major consumers, and what replanning or stopping under budget looks like.

The execution topology and the execution stages have to let a reader see the Search you actually wrote: every stage's real definition, execution family and visible uid, what it consumes and produces, its capability and budget, and every loop, repeated improvement, fan-out, fan-in, branch, and runtime replan the package really has, together with the design's global stop conditions. Choose the form yourself -- a TikZ diagram, a table, a rendered PNG, pseudocode, or plain prose -- and choose it because it explains the design, not because it looks like a paper. Whatever you draw must agree with the package you submitted; the reviewer reads both.

Cite every material factual claim that justifies a design decision. Ordinary BibTeX is the one bibliography for everything: an external paper, a dataset, a previous run, a package artifact, and your own pilot all enter it as ordinary entries such as `@misc`. Label an assumption as an assumption instead of citing it. A figure is allowed when it answers a question that changes or supports the design; a decorative figure is invalid.

